# Security.framework's export surface.
#
# Security links OpenSSL statically, which without this file leaks 10,728
# symbols - every X509_*, EVP_*, BIO_* and SSL_* in libcrypto and libssl - out
# of the framework. That is not cosmetic: an app that also carries its own
# OpenSSL can bind to ours instead of its own, and version-skewed crypto that
# silently half-works is a bad way to find out.
#
# It also gives tools/compat/coverage.py an allow-list to measure against, the
# same way libSystem.exports does, so Security's coverage can be tracked without
# a build.
#
# Anything not listed here is an implementation detail. Adding a symbol means
# committing to it.

# --- Secure Transport (TLS) -------------------------------------------------
_SSLClose
_SSLContextGetTypeID
_SSLCopyPeerCertificates
_SSLCopyPeerTrust
_SSLCreateContext
_SSLDisposeContext
_SSLGetAllowsAnyRoot
_SSLGetBufferedReadSize
_SSLGetClientCertificateState
_SSLGetConnection
_SSLGetEnableCertVerify
_SSLGetNegotiatedCipher
_SSLGetNegotiatedProtocolVersion
_SSLGetPeerDomainName
_SSLGetPeerDomainNameLength
_SSLGetPeerID
_SSLGetProtocolVersionMax
_SSLGetProtocolVersionMin
_SSLGetSessionOption
_SSLGetSessionState
_SSLHandshake
_SSLNewContext
_SSLRead
_SSLSetAllowsAnyRoot
_SSLSetAllowsExpiredCerts
_SSLSetAllowsExpiredRoots
_SSLSetConnection
_SSLSetEnableCertVerify
_SSLSetIOFuncs
_SSLSetPeerDomainName
_SSLSetPeerID
_SSLSetProtocolVersionMax
_SSLSetProtocolVersionMin
_SSLSetSessionOption
_SSLSetTrustedRoots
_SSLWrite

